            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">How do we choose a family meeting point after an earthquake?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Select two meeting points: one close to home for emergencies and one outside the neighborhood for situations where your area is unsafe. Both locations should be easily accessible, well-known to all family members, and away from buildings, power lines, and trees. Practice getting to both locations so everyone can find them without assistance.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">What are my children's school earthquake plans?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Contact your children's school directly to request their emergency response plan. Most schools conduct earthquake drills and have designated reunification procedures. Ask whether students shelter in place or evacuate, where the designated pickup area is located, and what identification you must present to collect your child. Provide the school with your emergency contact information and authorized pickup persons.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">What is an out-of-area emergency contact?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">An out-of-area contact is a friend or relative who lives in a different city or state that all family members call to report their status after a disaster. Long-distance phone calls often connect when local networks are congested or damaged. Choose someone everyone can reach easily and provide them with a written list of all family members' phone numbers.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">How does family reunification work after a major earthquake?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Follow a pre-established reunification sequence: first each person checks in at the neighborhood meeting point. If family members are not there within one hour, proceed to the secondary meeting location. If the secondary location is also empty, contact the out-of-area emergency contact. Each family member should carry a card with the meeting points, contact numbers, and reunification steps.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">How should I prepare young children for an earthquake?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Teach children the Drop, Cover, and Hold On technique through regular practice drills. Use age-appropriate language to explain earthquakes without creating fear. Practice finding safe spots in every room. Help children memorize your family meeting point and the out-of-area contacts phone number. Reassure them that being prepared means they know exactly what to do.</div></div>
